= To Fly Away

<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="color: #E3000F;">* Flavour Content *</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><span>This is a flavour event that does not have a direct impact on gameplay and provides extra content for immersion and narration. Though it's advised that you keep them for maximum enjoyment of the game, you can turn them off in the Options menu above.</span></span></span>

The pilot watched as the motor stopped. He tried strangling the steering wheel, to fly upwards and away, but nothing in the cockpit moved. He looked out the glass window and saw the battlefield underneath, with the trenches, the anti-aircraft guns (one of which must have been the one that shot his motor), and the tanks crushing the fertile ground. He looked again, towards the horizon this time, and saw the great sea. The Mediterranean it was, stretching far into the unknown, after which safety lay with the homeland. He wanted to go there, see his children again, and escape the atrocity of sudden death. He choked the steering wheel and lifted it upwards with all his remaining might. Yet he was beaten as he felt the plane diving downwards.

He had feared death a lot, yet feared his fear of death being known ever more. He volunteered in the air force, took the most unbelievable and stupid risks just to hide this fear. Yet as the end looked him in the eye now, he realized the futility of it all. He took out his wallet and looked at the picture of his children once more, for the last time.

As the plane warped through the clouds and the rocky ground became visible, the pilot began imagining. He wrapped the steering wheel and flew upwards. He passed through the clouds and left the fear of death and violence and bloodshed and many more fucking atrocities behind. He flew up through the blue sky that the redness behind the clouds could not penetrate. He flew, flew and flew and finally arrived at his imaginary homeland where there were no bombing campaigns, no anti-aircraft guns, no split families, no children left fatherless and where no young men were sent to die in preventable conflicts.

- @root: And thus he imagined himself on an iron bird.

